motion-lab-works-answer-average-speed-why = The ball keeps speeding up: part of gravity pulls it down along the groove. It rolls slowly at first and fast at the end. The first half of the path is the slow part, so its average speed is lower; over the whole path the fast second half adds in. Speeding up from rest, the path grows as the square of the time (l = a·t²/2), so the ball covers half the path not in half the time but in ≈ 71 % of it (1/√2) — and the average speed over the whole path is √2 ≈ 1.4 times greater. That is why an average speed always comes with the stretch it is for.
motion-lab-works-answer-average-speed-say = The ball speeds up — so the average speed differs!
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-why = The body goes round because the slanting thread pulls it not only up but also towards the centre. The circle is the same, but a short thread slants more, so a bigger share of the pull points to the centre — and the body has to go round faster for that pull to keep it just on the circle. It comes out T = 2π·√(h/g), h being the hand’s height above the circle: a shorter thread — the hand lower over the circle — a shorter period. It is the same law as for a thread pendulum — because it is a pendulum too, only it turns instead of swinging.
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-say = A shorter thread — quicker turns!
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-text = With a { $L } thread { $N } turns took { $t }: T = t/N ≈ { $value }, n = N/t ≈ { $value2 } rev/s, the body’s speed v = 2πR/T ≈ { $value3 } m/s.{" "}
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-text-2 = With a { $L } thread — { $t }: T ≈ { $value }.{" "}
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-text-3 = { $L }{ $L2 }The circle is the same, but a shorter thread slants more, and to go round the circle the body has to turn more often: the period depends on the thread (for a body on a thread T = 2π·√(h/g), h being the hand’s height above the circle). That is why the period is timed over many turns: one turn takes about a second, and a hand starts the stopwatch ≈ 0.1–0.2 s late. The body’s mass ({ $mass }) does not change the period: it depends only on how high the hand is over the circle.
motion-lab-works-answer-pendulum-why = Why does the mass not matter? The Earth pulls a heavier ball harder, but it is also harder to speed up — by just as much: both gravity and inertia are proportional to the mass, so a heavy ball moves like a light one (just as they fall alike when the air does not get in the way). Why does the amplitude hardly matter? With a wider swing the ball has farther to go, but the force bringing it back to the middle is larger too — proportional to how far it is out — so it also moves faster; at small angles the two cancel exactly. But the length matters: on a longer thread the same gravity brings the ball back more slowly — the restoring force is proportional to the displacement over the length. Hence T = 2π·√(l/g): a thread four times longer — twice the period.
motion-lab-works-answer-pendulum-say = Only the length sets a pendulum’s rhythm!
motion-lab-works-answer-pendulum-text = A { $L } pendulum: { $a1 } aside — T ≈ { $T }, { $a2 } — T ≈ { $T2 }, a { $m2 } ball — T ≈ { $T3 }: the same period. A { $short } pendulum — T ≈ { $T4 }: a thread four times shorter — half the period. The period of small swings depends on neither the amplitude nor the mass, only on the length: T = 2π·√(l/g) ≈ { $value } for { $L }. (A wider swing lengthens it by thousandths — a stopwatch in a hand will not notice.) So a 4 m pendulum would swing with a period of ≈ 4 s — twice as long as a metre one, whatever its ball.
