motion-lab-works-answer-average-speed-text = The ball covered the whole path l = { $value } in { $whole }: v = l/t ≈ { $v1 }. The first half — in { $half }: v ≈ { $v2 }. The ball keeps speeding up, so it covers the second half faster than the first, and the average speed on the whole path is greater — here { $v12 }. So an average speed always belongs to a stretch of the path. The stopwatch here reads exactly; by hand one starts and stops it ≈ 0.1–0.2 s late — that is why the textbook repeats the run three times and takes the mean time.
motion-lab-works-aside-arnold = The first person charged with speeding is taken to be the Englishman Walter Arnold. On 28 January 1896 he drove through the village of Paddock Wood in Kent at 8 mph (about 13 km/h), where only 2 mph (about 3 km/h) was allowed.
motion-lab-works-aside-hooke-conical = On 23 May 1666 Robert Hooke explained to the Royal Society in London why planets move along curves. What he showed was this very thing: a weight on a thread hung from the ceiling, going round in a circle. The weight, like a planet, is turned from a straight line at every moment by what pulls it to the centre.
motion-lab-works-aside-foucault = In March 1851 Léon Foucault hung a 28 kg ball on a 67 m wire in the Panthéon in Paris. The plane of its swing slowly turned, though nobody touched it: the Earth was turning under the pendulum. It was the first simple, direct evidence of the Earth’s rotation. An exact copy of that pendulum has swung under the Panthéon’s dome since 1995.
